+// LinkBackpressure makes a link endpoint's WouldBlock on write mean "not
+// sent, ask again": the segment is left unsent with SND.NXT unchanged and
+// the endpoint is queued for ResumeBlocked, instead of upstream's counting
+// it sent and recovering it by timer. It is how a bounded transmit backlog
+// (a per-socket queue limit, like Linux's TCP small queues) pushes back into
+// the sender rather than turning overload into loss.
+// SeparateZeroWindowProbe keeps a zero-window probe off the loss path: it is
+// still sent on the retransmit timer and backed off the same way, but it is
+// not counted as a timeout and does not collapse the congestion window or
+// reset the scoreboard. See retransmitTimerExpired.
+var SeparateZeroWindowProbe = true
+
+var LinkBackpressure bool
+
+// LinkTurn is how many segments an endpoint the link had refused may send
+// when the link resumes it (ResumeBlocked) before it goes back to the end of
+// the line, so that a full link is shared round-robin rather than by whoever
+// has the largest window. A segment here is a GSO packet of up to 64 KiB,
+// so one is already twenty times fq's quantum of two MTUs; with 500
+// connections in a queue's line, one segment each is an 11 ms round, two
+// is 22, and a keep-alive request's first byte waits about half a round.
+// Zero means no limit.
+var LinkTurn = 1
+
+// PreciseRTT makes the sender measure the round trip from its own clock (the
+// ACK's ingress time against the segment's send time) even when the peer
+// echoes a TCP timestamp. That option carries milliseconds, so on a
+// sub-millisecond path every sample taken from it reads as 0 or 1 ms: SRTT
+// settles at ~1 ms whatever the path really is, and congestion control —
+// whose window growth is per round trip — then behaves as if the link were
+// ten or twenty times longer than it is. Linux prefers the clock sample for
+// the same reason; upstream's guard here skips it whenever the timestamp
+// option is present.
+var PreciseRTT = true
